 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed edits are pushed to browsers over self-hosted WebSockets backed by replayable revision storage and browser reconnect/catch-up logic. Five-second HTTPS polling (simpler stateless operation, but higher repeated read load and multi-second lag) and a hosted realtime service (less connection operations work, but recurring vendor fees and provider coupling) were rejected to get fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor, accepting self-hosted connection capacity and reconnect operations. See [the change-delivery decision doc](decisions/change-delivery-transport.md).
